part.text:
I've created the Server Action in `app/actions.ts`.

The `createPost` action uses `revalidateTag('posts')` from `next/cache`. This function purges the cached data associated with the "posts" tag. When called within a Server Action, Next.js ensures that the underlying cache entry is removed. Consequently, the immediate subsequent render or navigation to a page that depends on this data will be a cache MISS, forcing a fresh fetch from the data source (mocked here). This guarantees the user will not see stale data.

Here is the file content:

```typescript
'use server'

import { revalidateTag } from 'next/cache'

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  const title = formData.get('title')
  const content = formData.get('content')

  // Simulate saving the post to a database
  // await db.post.create({ data: { title, content } })
  console.log('Creating post:', title)

  // Invalidate the 'posts' cache tag.
  // This guarantees that the next request (or the re-render of the current page)
  // will fetch fresh data, ensuring the user does not see stale content.
  revalidateTag('posts')
}
```
